Would a one-shot check after the window be worth it? The loop tolerates `Waiting`, and [`WaitForPodRunningInNamespace`](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/e76b659435be2cb29c680e2f29eefde3569cdc8b/test/e2e_node/probe_stress_test.go#L81) returns as soon as one container is up. So a container stuck in `Waiting` for the whole window (say `CreateContainerError`) never gets probed, and the test still passes with 49 probed containers.

Sergey's slow-CI concern shouldn't apply here. Every container has had at least the full 2 minutes to start, and one that started and died already fails the loop.

```suggestion
	}, probeStressWaitTime, 1*time.Second).Should(gomega.Succeed())

	// Every container has had the whole stress window to start. One still
	// Waiting here was never probed, so the loop above proved nothing for it.
	updatedPod, err := f.ClientSet.CoreV1().Pods(f.Namespace.Name).Get(ctx, pod.Name, metav1.GetOptions{})
	framework.ExpectNoError(err)
	gomega.Expect(updatedPod.Status.ContainerStatuses).To(gomega.HaveLen(len(pod.Spec.Containers)))
	for _, containerStatus := range updatedPod.Status.ContainerStatuses {
		gomega.Expect(containerStatus.State.Running).NotTo(gomega.BeNil(), "container %s never started: %+v", containerStatus.Name, containerStatus.State)
	}

	ginkgo.By("Test passed: no unexpected container restarts or terminations")
```

`go vet ./test/e2e_node/` is clean with this applied. In the kind runs in the review body, all 50 containers were `Running` at the end of the window for HTTP, TCP and gRPC, so it would have passed.

